+- id: echo-expands-what-printf-passes-through
+  kind: pattern
+  tier: 1
+  source: agent-emergent
+  nature: process
+  domain: process
+  statement: "A search pattern, fenced string or path is written to a file or piped onward with `printf '%s\\n'`, a quoted heredoc, or a file write - never with `echo`. The ambient shell here is zsh, whose `echo` expands backslash escapes by default, so a pasted pattern containing `\\x00`, `\\t`, `\\n` or `\\0` is silently TRANSFORMED rather than transmitted, and single quotes do not stop it - they stop the SHELL, while echo interprets the escapes itself. The consequence of a NUL is the part worth knowing, and it is worse than a visible warning: in this harness `grep` is a shell function execing the Claude Code binary with a fixed flag set INCLUDING `-I` (skip binary files), so one NUL anywhere makes the whole file invisible - no output, exit 1, which is byte-identical to an honest no-match. Measured 2026-07-30: the same file and pattern give 1 match at exit 0 as plain text, nothing at exit 1 once a single NUL is inserted, while `/usr/bin/grep` still reports the match. So the instrument converts a real hit into a clean-looking negative, and every grep-based check run over that file since the corruption is void rather than merely suspect. Trigger, readable: you are about to `echo` a string you did not author yourself, most often a regex or a quotation you are moving between a document and a report. Handle, cheap and mechanical: after generating any report text through the shell, run `file <path>` once - if it does not say `text`, discard every grep result taken from it. Distinct from shell-dialect-assumptions-in-agent-scripts, whose failure mode is an ABORT that a bash invocation fixes: this one completes successfully with corrupted output, and `bash -c` does not fix it because echo's escape handling varies by build and by shell option."
+  steelman: null
