+- id: an-edit-instruction-names-a-postcondition-not-a-position
+  kind: pattern
+  tier: 1
+  source: agent-emergent
+  nature: process
+  domain: process
+  statement: "An edit instruction that names a POSITION - insert between these two anchors, append after this line - silently assumes the state the file was in when the instruction was written, and it is applied later, to a file some other step may already have changed. An instruction that names a POSTCONDITION - after this edit the file holds exactly one `unsound = ` line - is checkable against ANY state and fails loudly on the wrong one. Trigger, readable: you are writing an edit instruction for someone else to apply, and it contains a preposition of place. Handle: keep the anchors, because they are how the editor finds the site, and add the postcondition beside them, because that is what tells the editor it got the right site. Where the file already contains an earlier version of the text being written - a fix round applying a corrected fence over the fence it replaces - the operation itself changes from insert to replace, and only the postcondition catches an instruction that did not notice."
