+- id: grep-output-order-is-not-stable-compare-the-set
+  kind: pattern
+  tier: 1
+  source: agent-emergent
+  nature: process
+  domain: process
+  statement: "A recursive grep's emitted ORDER is not stable in this environment, so any evidence practice that compares two runs as ordered text - a hash of the output, a diff against a previous paste, an eyeball of the first lines - reports a difference that is not one. Measured 2026-07-30: nine invocations of one `grep -rn` over an unchanged tree produced SEVEN distinct md5 sums, while piping the same output through `sort` produced one, nine times out of nine. Trigger, readable: you are about to hash, diff or byte-compare command output that lists matches across files. Handle: compare the SET - sort before hashing, or compare as lines-as-a-set - and say in the evidence line which you did. Nothing about the CONTENT is unstable; only the order is, which is what makes the false difference so convincing."
