   statement: "A guard, test, enumeration or check that a design document or plan has PROPOSED is not argued out again during the design or planning phase. It is removed only after it is built and MEASURED redundant. Rationale: the argument for dropping one always has the no-work-needed shape (proc-no-work-needed-check), and in the design phase that shape is uniquely UNFALSIFIABLE - a test that does not exist cannot be run to show it tests nothing, so agreement stands in for a measurement, and two agreeing agents are not one. Asymmetry: a redundant safeguard costs one redundant test; a wrongly-dropped one costs a silent hole in an unbuilt plan that nobody re-derives, because the document says the matter is settled. Keep the vacuity analysis and re-aim it: it becomes the recorded reason the safeguard is a candidate for removal at implementation time, under a trigger that names the experiment."
